WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.486
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:16.230
It was my privilege 26 years
ago to award you your bachelor's

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:22.020
degree, and 25 years ago,
your master's degree.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:25.260
It was soon recognized that
your first-rate performance

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:29.910
as a teacher was matched
by first-rate talent

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:32.130
as an administrator.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:36.090
And you were quickly co-opted
from one administrative job

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:37.890
after another.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.890
In fact, you hardly
had the opportunity

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.980
to warm the chair in
one post before you

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:45.210
were drafted for another.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:48.668
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:58.850
It's very nice to
have so many people

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:01.850
from so many institutions and
so many old friends who've

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:04.652
come back today to participate
in this celebration.

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:10.470
I got to know Paul Gray when I
was beginning graduate school.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:14.750
But we really became close
when I became his provost.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.810
I first met him through
his deep involvement

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:21.150
in undergraduate
education at MIT.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:25.040
He was a great friend,
a great colleague,

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.500
but also a very
great teacher for me.

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:34.640
I got involved as an athlete--

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.490
as a swimmer, as
rower, as a sailor.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:39.010
And I would often see him
and I would invite him

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:40.630
to our competitions.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:41.820
And he would come.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:45.400
And I remember he and Priscilla
coming in to a swimming meet,

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:48.790
and just sitting down, and
staying for the whole time.

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:52.750
He was a quietly very
brave, tenacious man,

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:57.490
loving and kind and
gentle and very focused

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:00.670
and always very engaged.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:05.560
He loved being around experts,
and taking advice from experts,

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:09.990
and learning from experts,
and sharing with experts.

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:15.600
He was the father who took
us hiking, biking, swimming,

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.282
sailing, skating.

00:02:18.282 --> 00:02:19.740
He's the one who
ran behind us when

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:22.031
we were learning to ride our
bikes until we could do it

00:02:22.031 --> 00:02:22.990
on our own.

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:24.240
There were no training wheels.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:25.240
You just figured it out.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:33.070
Paul began his MIT
life as a freshman.

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:36.540
Paul came to MIT in 1950,
so that's almost 70 years.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.090
That's just simply remarkable.

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:41.480
I cannot think of anybody else
who gave so much of his life,

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.630
who shared so much of his
life with this institute.

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:47.120
He's a very
down-to-earth individual.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:50.880
Very straightforward
man but very caring.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.365
He did so much for MIT.

00:02:52.365 --> 00:02:53.790
And he did it loyally.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:56.850
He did it morning,
noon, and night.

00:02:56.850 --> 00:03:00.430
He was so devoted to this
place and to the students

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:02.770
in such a genuine way.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:05.000
He was never in it for himself.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.120
He was always in it for MIT.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:14.730
Some years ago, in a
commencement in the rain,

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:18.460
a father was heard to say,
'after the soaking I've taken

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:21.178
for the last four years,
who minds a little rain?'

00:03:21.178 --> 00:03:23.598
[LAUGHTER AND CHEERING]

00:03:25.534 --> 00:03:27.470
But I have to say,
this is something else.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:35.950
In the competitive
sport of diving,

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:38.380
the various dives
are ranked according

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:41.560
to the degree of difficulty.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:45.490
When the performances of
different divers are equal,

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:47.800
the diver who chooses
the dive with the highest

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:50.980
degree of difficulty wins.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:53.820
Now by that measure alone,
you here today are winners.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:00.290
You have chosen and
successfully negotiated

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:03.950
a collegiate education having
what may be the nation's

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:06.252
highest degree of difficulty.

00:04:06.252 --> 00:04:08.130
[CHEERING]

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:10.475
He always called it
this special place.

00:04:10.475 --> 00:04:13.100
And I think for him, it
really was a special place

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:15.140
and a kind of home
unlike he'd ever

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:19.100
had before because it
satisfied him intellectually,

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.769
and it satisfied him,
I think, emotionally.

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:24.710
And when he was able to
connect his own family to it,

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:27.861
then I think that that
was sort of a full circle.

00:04:27.861 --> 00:04:31.010
MIT is best known for the
scientists and engineers

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:32.240
who have studied there.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:37.640
He was at MIT at a time when
engineering changed completely

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:41.600
from a largely empirical
practice of making

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:44.630
things and making them
work to a situation

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:48.260
where science and understanding
fundamentals affected

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:50.840
the design and the
implementation of so

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:52.950
many systems.

00:04:52.950 --> 00:05:00.410
This very gentle, quiet, fierce
man I know had to do battle

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:02.495
to get Whitehead
Institute established.

00:05:02.495 --> 00:05:05.420
People were terribly suspicious.

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:07.940
And you know, frankly, he
wasn't sure what it would be.

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:10.040
Nobody was.

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:11.180
It was an experiment.

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:13.457
And he took the risk.

00:05:13.457 --> 00:05:13.956
[CRACK]

00:05:14.455 --> 00:05:16.230
[CHEERING]

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:19.740
MIT and the biomedical
enterprise in Kendall Square

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:22.060
is unrivaled anywhere
in the world.

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:24.660
And so I do credit Paul
with setting us out

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:25.440
in that direction.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:26.790
So the Whitehead.

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:28.860
We now have the
Broad, and the Ragon,

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:31.980
the Koch Institute-- any
number of expressions

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:38.290
of the power of biology
to transform our world.

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:42.130
When I came to MIT,
which was in 1965,

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:45.850
I think maybe 2 or 3
percent of the students

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:48.650
were women, fewer than
that on the faculty.

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:52.150
It was very much a
male institution.

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:55.480
He was tremendously
committed to diversity,

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:58.900
increasing the
representation in the faculty

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:03.100
and in the student body of
women and of minorities.

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:07.750
The number of entering African
American students at MIT

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:13.570
went from about 3
to 5 per year to 57.

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:16.770
To this day, I mean, we're just
very proud of that momentum

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:19.900
that Paul brought to
the table on this issue.

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:25.120
MIT has a responsibility
to itself and to the nation

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:29.110
to be open and
indeed to reach out

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:33.610
to the most talented and
promising people, regardless

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:34.930
of race or sex.

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:36.420
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:38.170
None of us created all
the bias around us.

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:40.180
But we certainly inherit it.

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:42.100
And what I love
about President Gray

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:44.330
is that he stepped
right into that.

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:46.510
And as soon as he saw
it and understood it,

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:48.430
he decided to actually
do something about it.

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:52.318
[MUSIC - AARON COPLAND, "FANFARE
 FOR THE COMMON MAN"]

00:06:57.190 --> 00:07:00.190
During his inauguration,
what he wanted

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:04.210
as the walking-in music was
Aaron Copland's "Fanfare

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:06.850
for the Common Man."

00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:08.410
And that was him, you know?

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:14.190
He always was in touch with
his very modest beginnings

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:18.990
in a blue-collar family, where
the economy was a challenge.

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:21.936
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:26.334 --> 00:07:28.000
One of his fraternity
brothers, while he

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:30.700
was at MIT as a sophomore,
was dating a woman

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:33.274
at Wheaton College, where
my mother was a freshman.

00:07:33.274 --> 00:07:34.690
They introduced
my parents on what

00:07:34.690 --> 00:07:37.630
turned out to be a rather
successful blind date.

00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:39.460
It was very cold.

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:43.070
And they're walking
across the bridge,

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:46.710
and my mother doesn't
have any gloves.

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:51.730
And my father said, 'I've
got a pair of gloves.

00:07:51.730 --> 00:07:53.320
Why don't we do this.

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:55.360
I'll wear the left one.

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:56.980
You wear the right one.

00:07:56.980 --> 00:07:59.890
And we'll hold hands to
keep the middle ones warm.'

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:02.530
And they walked
across the bridge,

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:04.210
and they walked off
into the future.

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:07.660
That was the first day
of their relationship.

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:10.930
I thought, well, that's pretty
good for a nerd from New

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:13.110
Jersey.

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:17.140
A very clever use of a pair of
gloves to win the girl's heart.

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:30.080
It was the big blizzard of '78.

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:33.340
And no cars were allowed
on the road for a week.

00:08:33.340 --> 00:08:36.000
But MIT was not
going to shut down.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.830
So we started this bus line.

00:08:38.830 --> 00:08:44.120
And one of my favorite pictures
of Paul Gray is with his big

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:48.530
wolf skin or beaver skin hat--
you know, like a Russian hat--

00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:51.830
out in the parking lot
at MIT where the buses

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:53.720
came in, directing traffic.

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:56.480
I mean, he was
very much hands on.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:59.510
He looks incredibly happy,
actually, in the picture.

00:08:59.510 --> 00:09:01.760
He was just doing what
he could do, I think,

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:03.470
and having fun doing it.

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:07.400
I think he had a lot
of joy in what he did.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:10.460
He was a real proponent of
the Undergraduate Research

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:13.040
Opportunities Program, UROP.

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:15.560
It was dramatically
successful and found

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:18.950
a way for undergraduates to
interact with the faculty

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:21.120
in their research project.

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:22.910
This would be a way
in which students

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:25.580
could, for the first time
perhaps in their years at MIT,

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:30.140
come to terms with an unsolved
real problem, something

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:33.620
where they could make
a genuine contribution.

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:37.100
That became an integral part
of how MIT educates students.

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:40.130
So it was a huge thing for us.

00:09:40.130 --> 00:09:43.100
When we finished up all the
administrative and leadership

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:46.140
roles that he had at MIT, he
went back to the classroom.

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:47.780
He loved being in the classroom.

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:51.050
You turn the crank on
a three-by-three matrix

00:09:51.050 --> 00:09:59.220
and solve for the node voltages
e sub b, e sub c, and e sub d.

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:02.280
He was an absolutely
excellent teacher.

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:07.140
Clarity, complete concern with
the students' understanding.

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:10.760
Very much enjoyed
being in their company.

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:14.370
Professor Gray was
my academic adviser

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:16.550
when I was an
undergrad here at MIT.

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:19.250
And I remember he was a
really warm, very friendly,

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:21.794
giving adviser.

00:10:21.794 --> 00:10:23.210
At first, maybe
you would think it

00:10:23.210 --> 00:10:26.270
might be a little intimidating,
having your adviser be

00:10:26.270 --> 00:10:27.654
the ex-president of MIT.

00:10:27.654 --> 00:10:29.570
But that turned out not
to be the case at all.

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:32.975
Professor Gray was
extremely down to earth.

00:10:32.975 --> 00:10:36.830
He really understood
technological education,

00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:40.610
and what a technological
research university is really

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:42.590
about, and how precious it is.

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:47.730
One of the many involvements
that the Grays had

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:51.340
with students were
the senior dinners.

00:10:51.340 --> 00:10:55.780
We invited the whole class to
dinner, 60 or 70 at a time.

00:10:55.780 --> 00:11:00.370
All of us at MIT, we just
celebrate the opportunities

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:01.780
to be with those students.

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:04.450
They are so special.

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:08.290
Every semester on Registration
Day, we would meet with him.

00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:10.870
And then that evening, all
of us-- all of his advisees--

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:13.690
would go out to dinner with
him, always at the Royal East.

00:11:13.690 --> 00:11:16.960
You can imagine, there
was a big table, usually

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:20.650
in the back of the restaurant,
all filled with I'd say maybe

00:11:20.650 --> 00:11:23.480
about 10 or so advisees.

00:11:23.480 --> 00:11:28.850
Yours is the obligation to help
heal this society where healing

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:35.460
is required, to help strengthen
the nations where strengthening

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:39.930
is required, and to
help bring harmony

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:45.870
among the nations of this
world, wherever discord obtains.

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:50.970
If you have ever seen Paul's
official presidential portrait,

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:54.210
it is a portrait of
Paul and Priscilla.

00:11:54.210 --> 00:11:56.850
He was the first person to
have his spouse in the picture,

00:11:56.850 --> 00:11:57.840
right?

00:11:57.840 --> 00:11:59.490
And I think that,
for him, that was

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:02.100
a measure of his
devotion for her,

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:03.750
his appreciation
for all the things

00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:06.390
that my mother may have
sacrificed in order to help

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:11.777
him have the roles and the
opportunities that he had,

00:12:11.777 --> 00:12:13.610
and all the ways in
which she supported him.

00:12:13.610 --> 00:12:15.276
I think that that was
his way of saying,

00:12:15.276 --> 00:12:19.170
I wouldn't have been able to
do this without Priscilla.

00:12:19.170 --> 00:12:22.380
It was extraordinary
to watch them together,

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:25.110
how they worked together, how
they supported each other.

00:12:25.110 --> 00:12:26.800
They both had a
twinkle in their eye

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:29.880
that would kind of ping at you.

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:32.490
Just to know that there was
someone sort of watching over

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:34.800
us, making sure that things
generally are probably

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:36.480
going to be OK for
us, so I really

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:39.670
appreciated that very much.

00:12:39.670 --> 00:12:44.050
He is a man of
exceptional integrity.

00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:48.400
The humanness of the
man and of Priscilla

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:53.990
was so essential to the kind of
leadership he provided at MIT.

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:55.932
He cared about people.

00:12:55.932 --> 00:12:59.490
A person with
tremendous integrity,

00:12:59.490 --> 00:13:06.800
a person with tremendous
wisdom, thoughtful, calm.

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:10.290
An extraordinarily
forceful person

00:13:10.290 --> 00:13:14.760
but also with a surprisingly
gentle personality.

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:16.110
He was good at listening.

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:19.710
He was good at negotiating
and meeting people partway.

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:24.090
And he was good at imagining
what might be possible

00:13:24.090 --> 00:13:27.450
if this shifted or that changed
or we could move forward

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:29.280
together in this direction.

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:32.580
As you undertake this
next stage in your lives,

00:13:32.580 --> 00:13:35.670
I wish for you a life that
is rich in opportunities

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:40.710
to stretch your talents, your
interests, your imagination,

00:13:40.710 --> 00:13:44.070
and your vision of
this small planet

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:48.410
as a beautiful oasis,
generating peace.

00:13:48.410 --> 00:13:50.590
And as you depart from
this special place,

00:13:50.590 --> 00:13:52.560
I wish you Godspeed.

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:57.730
[MUSIC PLAYING]

